{
  "id": "ep231",
  "duration": 47.615,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.193,
      "text": "The Moon is actually spinning on its axis right now.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Full moon partially obscured by atmosphere.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Full_moon_partially_obscured_by_atmosphere.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.2,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.193,
      "end": 7.438,
      "text": "Many believe it stays still because we only ever see one face.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA Goddard Photo and Video - First Quarter (by).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA_Goddard_Photo_and_Video_-_First_Quarter_(by).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.2,
          "end": 5.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 5.333,
          "end": 7.433,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 7.438,
      "end": 12.388,
      "text": "If it didn't rotate, we would see the lunar far side every single month.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Far side of the Moon close up for Lunar Crater Radio Telescope.png",
        "credit": "Wikideas1",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Far_side_of_the_Moon_close_up_for_Lunar_Crater_Radio_Telescope.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 7.433,
          "end": 9.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 9.867,
          "end": 12.4,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.388,
      "end": 17.308,
      "text": "Instead, Earth\u2019s gravity has spent billions of years braking the Moon\u2019s spin.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon apsidal precession.png",
        "credit": "Rfassbind",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_apsidal_precession.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.4,
          "end": 17.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.308,
      "end": 23.403,
      "text": "This invisible grip created solid tidal bulges, using friction to slow the rotation.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "PSM V74 D529 Relationship between tidal forces the moon and latitude.png",
        "credit": "Unknown authorUnknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V74_D529_Relationship_between_tidal_forces_the_moon_and_latitude.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.3,
          "end": 23.4,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.403,
      "end": 30.028,
      "text": "Now, the Moon completes one rotation in about twenty-seven point three days to match its orbit.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Once in a Super Blue Moon (LROC1309).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/GSFC/Arizona State University",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Once_in_a_Super_Blue_Moon_(LROC1309).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.4,
          "end": 30.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.028,
      "end": 36.498,
      "text": "This cosmic anchor is so powerful it even slows Earth\u2019s day by two milliseconds every century.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.033,
          "end": 36.5,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.498,
      "end": 42.643,
      "text": "Our planet is losing this tug-of-war as the Moon spirals away by four centimeters every year.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Apollo 11 Lunar Laser Ranging Experiment.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_11_Lunar_Laser_Ranging_Experiment.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.5,
          "end": 40.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 40.6,
          "end": 42.633,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 42.643,
      "end": 47.265,
      "text": "It is a perpetual, balanced cycle that explains why our neighbor is turning.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Full moon partially obscured by atmosphere.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Full_moon_partially_obscured_by_atmosphere.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 42.633,
          "end": 47.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
